WEBVTT

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:19.740
The monster of the 
Showa Era, Washizu Iwao.

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:23.560
He's amassed untold riches and power,

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:27.710
and now he uses it to gamble on 
young people's lives for his pleasure.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:30.060
In order to hunt down this monster,

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.710
Akagi, the genius from the 
darkness, stands up to him.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:37.310
Akagi will bet his very life's blood.

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:40.300
Washizu will bet the 
remainder of his fortune.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:44.050
The battle that may bring 
either to ruin has begun.

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:48.130
<b>Episode 17</b>

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:48.130
<b>The Proof of Genius</b>

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:55.060
Washizu's riichi misses its mark.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:02:01.050
Akagi takes the 1,000 points, 
and the corresponding one million yen.

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:08.980
East Hand 2.

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:10.370
The dealer is Washizu.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.240
After losing the previous hand, 
his opening hand isn't good.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.670
Compared to Washizu's bad starting 
hand and bad draws, Akagi is doing well.

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:22.980
In only the fourth turn...

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.060
<i>Good.</i>

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:26.250
<i>One away from tenpai.</i>

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:28.680
It's not an impressive hand,

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.580
but winning it would mean bumping 
Washizu out of the dealer's spot.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:33.990
Then on the fifth turn...

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:37.230
Yasuoka draws a red dragon.

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:39.970
<i>If I make him call this red dragon,</i>

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:43.610
<i>Akagi will probably be in tenpai 
with a 2 or 5 of bamboo wait.</i>

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:44.470
<i>In that case...</i>

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:47.210
<i>All right, Akagi.</i>

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:48.940
<i>Call it.</i>

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:51.180
Pon.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:53.910
<i>Good.</i>

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.130
This game is between 
Akagi and Washizu alone.

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:02.730
Since his own points don't matter,

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:04.830
it makes sense that Yasuoka 
would try to help Akagi.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:09.680
In this instance, he lets Akagi call 
the red dragon to get him into tenpai.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:11.230
He then quickly deals into his hand.

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:12.730
Ron.

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:15.770
Red dragon, one dora. 2,000.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:22.270
<i>You trash...</i>

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:25.640
<i>You're not worth my time.</i>

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:28.280
<i>Even if you wanted me 
out of the dealer's spot,</i>

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:32.550
<i>your hand was ideal because 
all you had to do was wait</i>

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:36.290
<i>for Yasuoka to deal into it 
with the red dragon.</i>

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:40.520
<i>And you took that for nothing 
but a quick 2,000 win.</i>

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:43.770
<i>You're an idiotic coward.</i>

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:46.530
<i>Since you're so slow, 
let me teach you a lesson...</i>

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:51.450
<i>on the most effective way 
to play Washizu Mahjong.</i>

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:53.820
<i>The strongest weapon...</i>

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:55.830
Riichi.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:01.350
<i>This one's rough.</i>

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:03.630
<i>He has so many black tiles.</i>

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:05.020
<i>If he's going for a mentanpin,</i>

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:07.810
<i>there are any number of possible waits.</i>

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:12.360
<i>In an extreme case, 
he could even have a chiitoitsu.</i>

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:18.910
You moron.

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:23.270
You don't even have the 
right to worry about it.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:24.770
Hey.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:27.090
Ron.

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:31.510
Riichi, ippatsu, tanyao.

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:35.480
And the reverse dora is...

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:37.230
I got one.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:39.450
So it's a mangan.

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:41.160
Do you understand now?

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:44.690
The terror that is Washizu Mahjong?

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:49.420
The riichi and the instant 
deal of the winning tile is

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:52.930
one of the most powerful ways 
to team up in Washizu Mahjong.

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:58.040
Even with the cheapest hand, 
a riichi ippatsu is worth two han.

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:01.440
If a reverse dora is added, 
it instantly becomes a mangan.

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:06.370
I'm 100% certain you've got 
no chance of winning

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:08.440
with that gutless playing style.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:11.750
You may avoid direct hits from me,

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:14.450
but you can't keep me from outranking you.

00:05:14.450 --> 00:05:16.820
Come on, boy.

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:19.950
Attack me, if you want to survive.

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:25.760
But if you're too scared, 
just sit back and die.

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:30.430
Well, even if you decide to attack now,

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:36.200
that mangan put me 
2,000 points ahead of you.

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:38.380
If you want to survive,

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:41.400
you have to attack, and attack, 
and get ahead of me.

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:45.530
Discard the tile that will decide your fate.

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:48.140
Close the differential between us.

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:54.040
By making it through that carnage, 
that danger, you will see

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:59.160
the faint light of your hope to survive.

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:03.970
He is right. With this type of team play,

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:05.920
it is impossible to stay ahead

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:08.460
with defensive playing 
or winning quick hands.

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:12.280
That is to say, little tricks 
are absolutely pointless.

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:15.890
Since assisting each other is 
allowed in this one-on-one battle,

00:06:15.890 --> 00:06:17.180
he will eventually lose.

00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:22.390
Declaring riichi and then having another 
deal immediately into your hand

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:24.250
is not only efficient,

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:26.220
but one could say that it is standard.

00:06:28.010 --> 00:06:32.460
This strategy has no less 
than three strong points.

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:36.390
First, as demonstrated by Washizu,

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:39.430
by adding 2 han with the 
riichi ippatsu combo,

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:43.200
one or two reverse dora will suffice 
to make it an expensive mangan hand.

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:45.870
The second is its speed.

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:51.210
The time between a tenpai and 
a win is completely eliminated.

00:06:52.510 --> 00:06:55.670
And so long as your teammate has 
that tile, your wait can be anything.

00:06:56.110 --> 00:07:00.950
A side wait, a closed wait, 
or even a single wait.

00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:03.950
All of them can lead to an ippatsu win.

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:07.460
And most importantly, 
that sense of safety.

00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:10.960
The moment you declare riichi, 
the next player deals into your hand.

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:15.180
In other words, you get the 
benefits of declaring a riichi

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:18.540
without the danger of having 
to immediately discard anything

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:20.160
that is not your own winning tile.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:23.710
Only the tile you discard when 
declaring your riichi must pass.

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:26.710
It's expensive, fast, and safe.

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:30.210
In other words, the most effective strategy.

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:36.350
<i>He's got no choice now 
but to join him in the ring.</i>

00:07:39.850 --> 00:07:42.210
<i>It has its dangers,</i>

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:46.300
<i>but he has to play along with this duel.</i>

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:47.360
<i>Akagi...</i>

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:53.950
Akagi is one tile from tenpai.

00:07:56.930 --> 00:07:59.110
<i>The problem is what comes next.</i>

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:06.620
<i>Is he in tenpai?</i>

00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:09.020
<i>If he's not declaring a riichi now,</i>

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:11.190
<i>it probably means his 
underling doesn't have</i>

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:13.520
<i>his winning tile in his hand.</i>

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:19.190
<i>He can always do it once his 
underling draws the tile he needs.</i>

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:24.430
<i>But if Akagi plays into his hand 
before that, he'll get a direct hit.</i>

00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:26.360
<i>He'll just have to say "ron."</i>

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:28.750
<i>It's a rock-solid strategy.</i>

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:33.670
<i>He should just assume that Washizu 
is in tenpai and play safely.</i>

00:08:33.670 --> 00:08:35.540
<i>From what I can see,</i>

00:08:35.540 --> 00:08:38.180
<i>it looks like the 4 and 7 
of bamboo are dangerous.</i>

00:08:39.430 --> 00:08:41.820
In this hand, the dora is the 6 of bamboo,

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:45.680
so with Washizu's 6 of bamboo, 
his wait is most likely for the 4 and 7,

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:49.190
or the 5 and 8, of bamboo. 
These are the danger tiles.

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:53.680
He may also possibly be using 
two 6 of bamboo as his pair.

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:58.600
There is another danger area 
around the 6 of circles,

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:03.940
implying a 4 and 7, or 5 and 8 wait, 
making those tiles very risky.

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:08.100
<i>He's in tenpai.</i>

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:11.410
<i>Now he's got a 5 or 8 of characters wait.</i>

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:15.920
<i>But of all things, he'll have 
to discard the 7 of bamboo.</i>

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:17.920
<i>That's the worst!</i>

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:22.150
It's an extremely risky tile, and 
the exact tile he's waiting on.

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:25.470
<i>You've got to keep that tile, Akagi.</i>

00:09:31.580 --> 00:09:33.130
<i>That's crazy!</i>

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:35.250
<i>That's the...</i>

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:40.340
Did you deal into his hand?

00:09:41.090 --> 00:09:42.660
Did you?

00:09:54.190 --> 00:09:55.360
<i>It passed!</i>

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:02.880
<i>Why could he deal such a risky tile?</i>

00:10:05.470 --> 00:10:08.300
<i>Did he somehow know that it was safe?</i>

00:10:09.070 --> 00:10:11.800
<i>That the 7 of bamboo would pass?</i>

00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:20.930
<i>The 5 and the 8 of bamboo passed, too.</i>

00:10:20.930 --> 00:10:25.220
<i>Which means he probably has 
the two dora as the head</i>

00:10:25.220 --> 00:10:28.770
<i>and he's waiting on the 4 and 7 
of circles, or 5 and 8 of circles.</i>

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:31.090
<i>Since he knows what 
Washizu's waiting on,</i>

00:10:31.090 --> 00:10:33.100
<i>he doesn't have to worry about 
dealing into his hand anymore.</i>

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:38.350
<i>He just has to avoid the risky 
tiles, stay vigilant, and win.</i>

00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:40.150
<i>If he wins with the 
5 and 8 of characters,</i>

00:10:40.150 --> 00:10:43.170
<i>Akagi will continue to be the dealer.</i>

00:10:43.710 --> 00:10:45.460
<i>He can catch up to Washizu.</i>

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:47.170
<i>And if he draws it himself, 
he can take the lead.</i>

00:10:47.650 --> 00:10:53.390
<i>He can bring the luck that's gone 
to Washizu back to his side.</i>

00:10:56.260 --> 00:10:59.220
<i>He had to draw the 5 
of circles, of all things.</i>

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:02.480
<i>This is one of those last four risky tiles.</i>

00:11:03.260 --> 00:11:05.570
<i>He has no choice but 
to hold on to this one.</i>

00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:08.100
<i>It's still only East Hand 4.</i>

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:09.930
<i>He can't take that risk.</i>

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:13.000
<i>It would be reckless to 
discard that tile now.</i>

00:11:20.570 --> 00:11:21.440
<i>You're crazy!</i>

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:24.940
<i>What the hell are you thinking?</i>

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:27.950
<i>That's too much.</i>

00:11:30.230 --> 00:11:31.690
I see.

00:11:31.690 --> 00:11:35.870
I guess you're not completely worthless.

00:11:37.900 --> 00:11:40.460
Even if they notice the 
second discard pile,

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:43.960
they usually don't get so bold...

00:11:45.620 --> 00:11:47.700
Usually.

00:11:49.770 --> 00:11:53.330
At this point, Washizu is waiting 
on the 4 and 7 of circles.

00:11:55.310 --> 00:11:57.200
<i>The second discard pile?</i>

00:11:57.200 --> 00:11:58.710
<i>What's he talking about?</i>

00:12:07.130 --> 00:12:08.750
<i>I get it.</i>

00:12:08.750 --> 00:12:11.050
<i>That's what he's talking about.</i>

00:12:11.900 --> 00:12:15.790
Why does Washizu not declare a 
riichi despite being in tenpai?

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:18.000
There could be only one reason:

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:20.640
His underling doesn't 
have his winning tile.

00:12:21.770 --> 00:12:23.310
Seen another way,

00:12:23.310 --> 00:12:27.300
all of Washizu's underling's tiles 
become safe tiles, as well.

00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:29.790
It is a veritable "second discard pile."

00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:35.540
<i>Why didn't I notice that sooner?</i>

00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:41.780
<i>Akagi used this to discard the 
7 of bamboo and the 5 of circles.</i>

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:46.320
<i>It's amazing that he could notice a detail 
like that under this kind of pressure.</i>

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:48.290
<i>That's Akagi for you.</i>

00:12:48.750 --> 00:12:53.530
<i>What is truly amazing, 
though, is that he was able</i>

00:12:53.530 --> 00:12:57.030
<i>to bet his life on this observation.</i>

00:12:58.670 --> 00:13:02.240
Washizu is also undoubtedly feeling 
some admiration for this audacity.

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:08.170
Because the second discard pile could 
also potentially be used to lure Akagi

00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:12.040
into dealing the winning tile, 
using the transparent tiles as bait.

00:13:13.580 --> 00:13:18.020
Yet, Akagi threw all those doubts away.

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:22.520
<i>That is exactly what makes him</i>

00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:25.260
<i>Akagi Shigeru.</i>

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:28.130
<i>He can win.</i>

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:32.920
<i>In this hand, he discarded two 
tiles that should have been</i>

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:35.460
<i>impossible to discard, 
and maintained his tenpai.</i>

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:41.090
<i>It might not be much, but he can 
still win this hand before Washizu does.</i>

00:13:42.110 --> 00:13:43.740
<i>He can draw the winning tile.</i>

00:13:54.820 --> 00:13:56.990
Tanyao, tsumo, two dora. Mangan.

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:01.720
This is what Mahjong is about.

00:14:02.140 --> 00:14:03.870
You can play a perfect game...

00:14:03.870 --> 00:14:07.730
no, better than perfect...

00:14:07.730 --> 00:14:10.020
and you'll still fall short.

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:13.150
You can give your very best and still die.

00:14:14.380 --> 00:14:16.870
It's Mahjong where, 
no matter how hard you fight,

00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:19.240
you die when your time comes.

00:14:19.640 --> 00:14:21.980
Is that not so, Akagi?

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:27.270
The game is going in your favor.

00:14:27.270 --> 00:14:29.260
You thought you've made it.

00:14:29.260 --> 00:14:32.490
And yet your opponent draws the 
winning tile, with maddening ease.

00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:35.130
It is not a question 
of talent nor strategy.

00:14:35.780 --> 00:14:38.440
Once the opponent draws the 
winning tile, it is over.

00:14:39.530 --> 00:14:43.500
Even Akagi is powerless to stop this.

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:47.020
Akagi has 26,000 points.

00:14:47.510 --> 00:14:49.360
Washizu has 40,000.

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:52.740
That's a difference of 14,000 points, and...

00:14:52.740 --> 00:14:55.740
It's time to pay the bonus.

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:00.180
Blood is drawn.

00:15:05.820 --> 00:15:09.490
Whenever Akagi and 
Washizu exchange points,

00:15:09.490 --> 00:15:12.330
whether through a tsumo 
or the obvious direct hit,

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:15.380
it costs Washizu one million yen,

00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:22.240
and Akagi 100 CCs of blood 
for every 1,000 points.

00:15:23.180 --> 00:15:25.070
This time it was 4,000 points,

00:15:25.070 --> 00:15:27.470
which means 400 CCs of blood.

00:15:28.590 --> 00:15:33.980
He must fill one 250 CC tube 
and add 150 CCs to another.

00:15:35.930 --> 00:15:37.520
Now we're starting.

00:15:37.520 --> 00:15:40.280
The real game has just begun.

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:41.840
That's right.

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:44.990
This is Washizu's vampire Mahjong,

00:15:44.990 --> 00:15:47.230
demanding the very 
blood of his opponents.

00:15:47.770 --> 00:15:49.970
That reality has been set into motion.

00:15:52.210 --> 00:15:53.470
<i>This is harsh.</i>

00:15:53.990 --> 00:15:56.240
<i>This is too harsh.</i>

00:15:56.790 --> 00:16:02.070
<i>If he gets three more tsumo like this, 
that will already be 1,600 CCs.</i>

00:16:02.070 --> 00:16:03.480
<i>He'll be barely conscious.</i>

00:16:03.860 --> 00:16:08.750
<i>By the fifth withdrawal, he'll have 
reached the fatal amount of 2,000 CCs.</i>

00:16:08.750 --> 00:16:11.480
<i>He can't possibly keep this 
up for six East-South matches.</i>

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:12.720
<i>There's no way.</i>

00:16:13.090 --> 00:16:14.150
Yes.

00:16:14.150 --> 00:16:17.440
The gap between Akagi and 
Washizu is 14,000 points.

00:16:17.950 --> 00:16:19.720
Herein lies the problem.

00:16:20.450 --> 00:16:22.490
Unless Akagi regains the lead,

00:16:22.490 --> 00:16:24.160
at the end of this hand, 
he will have to pay

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:26.070
the ranking bonus of 20,000 points,

00:16:26.070 --> 00:16:29.230
that is to say, to have 
2,000 CCs of blood drawn.

00:16:29.660 --> 00:16:32.170
That is certain death.

00:16:32.170 --> 00:16:35.670
<i>That'll be the death of him.</i>

00:16:36.620 --> 00:16:39.730
A dark and ominous cloud 
hangs over the table.

00:16:39.730 --> 00:16:41.680
The game moves into the South round.

00:16:45.950 --> 00:16:47.850
<i>It's laughable.</i>

00:16:47.850 --> 00:16:50.340
<i>He wanted this reckless 
rate of 2,000 CCs of blood</i>

00:16:50.340 --> 00:16:54.190
<i>being worth 20,000 points, 
and look at him now.</i>

00:16:54.190 --> 00:16:55.190
<i>Trash.</i>

00:16:57.740 --> 00:17:01.880
<i>Though, he did show a little aptitude.</i>

00:17:01.880 --> 00:17:08.290
<i>I was impressed by his bold 
utilization of the second discard pile.</i>

00:17:08.910 --> 00:17:11.580
<i>But it was nothing more 
than a little trick.</i>

00:17:12.140 --> 00:17:15.120
<i>That's not the way to 
grab hold of this game.</i>

00:17:15.980 --> 00:17:21.920
<i>He's the one who chose 2,000 
points over a mangan hand.</i>

00:17:22.700 --> 00:17:27.860
<i>That was the moment you drove 
the luck from your side.</i>

00:17:28.280 --> 00:17:30.260
<i>You may be able to avoid 
dealing into my hands,</i>

00:17:30.260 --> 00:17:32.070
<i>but that's all you'll be able to do.</i>

00:17:33.530 --> 00:17:36.370
<i>Once you've lost the favor of 
Lady Luck, you can't get it back.</i>

00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:39.600
<i>The good draws will 
come to me first.</i>

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:42.620
<i>That's inevitable.</i>

00:17:44.890 --> 00:17:49.700
<i>Dark demons live in 
the world of Mahjong,</i>

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:56.550
<i>and what they appreciate is maturity 
and elegance more than childish tricks.</i>

00:17:56.550 --> 00:18:01.050
<i>That is to say, my style.</i>

00:18:06.910 --> 00:18:09.300
<i>I'm already close to tenpai.</i>

00:18:10.240 --> 00:18:12.030
<i>That's no fun.</i>

00:18:13.180 --> 00:18:15.070
If he deals the 5 of characters, 
he will be in tenpai.

00:18:15.590 --> 00:18:17.540
It would be a tanpin, 
sanshoku, with one dora.

00:18:18.510 --> 00:18:20.090
If he declares riichi,

00:18:20.090 --> 00:18:23.590
his underling will be able to supply him 
with the winning 2 of circles right away,

00:18:25.040 --> 00:18:27.470
netting him the ippatsu bonus 
and giving him a haneman.

00:18:27.470 --> 00:18:29.920
If he gets even one reverse dora, 
it increases to a baiman.

00:18:30.470 --> 00:18:32.530
It is the ideal shape.

00:18:32.750 --> 00:18:34.490
<i>This is pointless.</i>

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:37.720
<i>This win will end it all.</i>

00:18:38.510 --> 00:18:40.920
Currently, there is a 14,000 
point gap between them.

00:18:40.920 --> 00:18:44.740
With this win, that gap 
would become 26,000 points,

00:18:44.740 --> 00:18:46.800
with the potential to rise to 30,000.

00:18:47.470 --> 00:18:50.400
Reversing the situation after 
that would be impossible.

00:18:52.350 --> 00:18:54.440
Akagi's death would be certain.

00:18:54.970 --> 00:18:57.100
<i>So that's it?</i>

00:18:57.100 --> 00:18:58.770
<i>That was no fun.</i>

00:19:00.210 --> 00:19:05.480
<i>But I guess that's street 
kid Mahjong for you.</i>

00:19:07.470 --> 00:19:10.120
Riichi.

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:12.360
Nope.

00:19:15.010 --> 00:19:17.360
Tanyao, one dora. 2,600.

00:19:19.070 --> 00:19:21.330
<i>A single wait for the 5 of characters?</i>

00:19:21.710 --> 00:19:23.950
<i>He took the 5 of characters 
that would naturally come out</i>

00:19:23.950 --> 00:19:26.830
<i>if I were to go for a sanshoku.</i>

00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:29.750
<i>Was that on purpose?</i>

00:19:30.490 --> 00:19:31.980
<i>Coincidence?</i>

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:35.520
<i>No, he planned it.</i>

00:19:36.660 --> 00:19:38.760
<i>Just before, he discarded the 6 of circles,</i>

00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:41.860
<i>which proves this 5 of characters 
wait was no coincidence.</i>

00:19:41.860 --> 00:19:44.990
<i>It was intentional.</i>

00:19:45.950 --> 00:19:48.270
Had he kept the 6 of circles in his hand,

00:19:48.270 --> 00:19:50.680
his tenpai would have looked like this.

00:19:51.880 --> 00:19:55.050
He would have been had a 4-way 
wait for 1, 4, 7 or 6 of circles.

00:19:55.680 --> 00:19:59.600
Yet he threw away that four-way 
wait for this single 5 of characters wait.

00:20:00.770 --> 00:20:02.840
I get it.

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:07.770
Th-Thanks.

00:20:11.370 --> 00:20:12.580
Pretty good.

00:20:13.850 --> 00:20:16.590
Any other kind of play would be boring.

00:20:16.940 --> 00:20:19.600
It would have been horribly dull if you

00:20:19.600 --> 00:20:22.090
hadn't shown at least this 
little flash of talent.

00:20:23.660 --> 00:20:25.330
<i>Fight back.</i>

00:20:25.330 --> 00:20:28.430
<i>Come back at me with 
everything you've got.</i>

00:20:30.790 --> 00:20:33.640
<i>Show me that very last 
shred of resistance</i>

00:20:33.640 --> 00:20:35.860
<i>when I've got you backed into the corner.</i>

00:20:36.640 --> 00:20:45.710
<i>That, for me, is the ultimate pleasure!</i>

00:20:47.950 --> 00:20:50.670
The genius from the 
darkness, Akagi Shigeru.

00:20:51.290 --> 00:20:53.720
The monster of the 
Showa Era, Washizu Iwao.

00:20:54.990 --> 00:20:57.980
Two monsters that live 
in the world of shadows.

00:20:57.980 --> 00:20:59.730
Their deathmatch continues.

00:21:00.420 --> 00:21:05.970
Who will be the one to fall into 
the pitch-black void of obliteration?

00:22:16.280 --> 00:22:19.270
Next time, "The Cursed Tiles."

00:22:17.750 --> 00:22:20.550
<b>Next episode</b>

00:22:17.750 --> 00:22:20.550
<b>The Cursed Tiles</b>
